## What is failing and why

The failing subject is a **package build failure**, not an assertion failure:

```
# fixture/buildcodecompile/tests/suite_test [fixture/buildcodecompile/tests/suite.test]
tests/suite/multiply_test.go:13:14: undefined: calc.Multiply
FAIL	fixture/buildcodecompile/tests/suite [build failed]
```

`tests/suite/multiply_test.go` imports `fixture/buildcodecompile/services/calc` and calls `calc.Multiply(2, 3)`. The production package at `services/calc/calc.go` (module `fixture/buildcodecompile`, see `go.mod`) declares only `Add`, and its trailing comment explicitly states that `Multiply` is the planted, production-side defect. Because Go compiles an external test package as a single unit, the one undefined identifier takes down the whole `suite_test` package: `TestAddSumsItsArguments` and `TestCanaryRecordsEachSuiteRun` never run either. One build error, one failing set — fixing the single missing declaration clears all of it.

